Why this exists
---------------

tseg ships an optional backend, tseg/models/sam3_backend.py, whose only
purpose is to call SAM 3. SAM 3 is distributed under a custom licence
that imposes field-of-use restrictions, so it is not GPL-compatible and
is not an open-source licence under the Open Source Definition.

Without this permission, distributing a module written specifically to
link non-free code would be, at best, a contested reading of the GPL.
The exception removes the ambiguity rather than relying on anyone's
interpretation of it.

Scope
-----

This permission covers linking only. It does not relicense SAM 3, does
not extend to any other non-GPL-compatible dependency, and does not
apply to any part of tseg other than what is required to combine the two.

Nothing in tseg depends on SAM 3 by default. It is an opt-in extra
(`pip install tseg[sam3]`), every core workflow runs without it, and the
licence sweep in CI fails if it ever becomes a required dependency.

If you would rather avoid the question entirely, do not install the
`sam3` extra. Tree bootstrapping uses DeepForest (MIT) and rare-class
mining uses DINOv2 embeddings (Apache-2.0); both are fully open source.
